{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Be gracious to me</b> — It is possible that the reason for the pause is: for I will rejoice in the kindness that You did with me in days past, and You did not deliver me over; only now my distress is very great and not broad. Many interpreted that this psalm concerns an illness that befell him." "<b>The reason for \"is consumed with grief\"</b> — on account of the enemy's anger being upon me, I am entirely consumed, as though a moth ate me. And he likened grief to being in the heart because of the body's heat." "<b>The reason for \"my iniquity\"</b> — that the food that the sick person sees becomes loathsome to him, \"all food his soul loathes.\"" "<b>The reason for \"my soul\"</b> — the desiring soul, whose faculty is rooted in the liver, and thus the verse; or the eating soul." "<b>And \"my bowels\"</b> — an allusion to the upper intestine, where the food is. And behold, the reason is that the body's strength is weak, \"like a garment the moth has eaten.\""], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:29:06.209011607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}